-- DICT-SEMANTICS §5 "Arg-tag dispatch is an OPTIMIZATION, not a semantics":
-- "Overlap narrows this side condition sharply: a head tag alone does not
-- separate overlapping instances below one constructor -- a `List` tag cannot
-- distinguish the `List Int` instance from the `List a` instance -- so for any
-- class with such overlap, arg-tag selection at that constructor is UNSOUND, not
-- merely incomplete."
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): both calls pass a value whose runtime head tag is
-- `List`, and each goal is matched by at least the parametric instance:
--   `tag ([1] : List Int)`      -> match = {List a, List Int}, min⊑ = List Int  => 99
--   `tag (["a"] : List String)` -> match = {List a} only                        => 10
-- An arg-tag evaluator sees a `List` cons cell in both cases and must answer the
-- same way twice; whichever it picks, one line is wrong. This is the minimal
-- statement of the §5/§7 argument, with no nesting to confound it.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all ACCEPT and print 99 then 10. CONFORMANT.
--
-- ⚠️ Agreement across engines is evidence here ONLY because the two lines
-- disagree with EACH OTHER. Three engines agreeing on one line would prove
-- nothing -- several known S0s have every engine equally wrong. And note the
-- sibling fixture s3-min-fully-general-sibling.mdk (**#1128**): replacing
-- `impl Tag (List a)` below with the FULLY GENERAL `impl Tag a` makes this exact
-- discriminator fail silently on both engines. The two rows differ in one token.
interface Tag a where
  tag : a -> Int

impl Tag (List a) where
  tag _ = 10

impl Tag (List Int) where
  tag _ = 99

main =
  println (tag ([1] : List Int))
  println (tag (["a"] : List String))
